# tarishsharingd — the unprivileged half of Tarish.
#
# This domain deliberately has NO capabilities. Everything that parses input
# from another device runs here, so the rule of thumb is: if a permission is
# only needed to reconfigure the network or touch the radio, it belongs to
# tarishd, not to this domain. Compare tarishd.te — it holds NET_ADMIN and NET_RAW
# and parses nothing.
#
# Network access comes from group membership (`inet`) plus an explicit, measured set of
# socket rules -- NOT from a capability, and no longer from net_domain(). That is the whole
# point: this process can talk to a peer over TCP and UDP and can do nothing else. It has
# no raw IP sockets, no ICMP, and no way to read the routing table.

type tarishsharingd, domain, coredomain;
type tarishsharingd_exec, file_type, exec_type, system_file_type;

# The binder service the client app binds.
type tarish_service, service_manager_type;

init_daemon_domain(tarishsharingd)

# The network access this daemon has, MEASURED rather than granted wholesale.
#
# This was net_domain(tarishsharingd) until 2026-09-24. That macro is a big grant, and the
# list matters because none of it was ever argued for individually:
#
#   self:tcp_socket                            create_stream_socket_perms
#   self:{ icmp_socket udp_socket rawip_socket }   create_socket_perms
#   node_type:{ icmp rawip tcp udp }:*         node_bind
#   port_type:tcp_socket                       name_connect, name_bind
#   port_type:udp_socket                       name_bind
#   self:netlink_route_socket  { create read getattr write setattr lock append connect
#                                getopt setopt shutdown nlmsg_read nlmsg } + allowxperm
#
# So the most exposed process in Tarish -- the one that parses input from another device --
# could open raw IP sockets, open ICMP sockets, and read the routing table. It needs none
# of that. tarish_app was cut from the same macro down to four rules; this domain is the
# privileged half and had a better claim to being narrowed first.
#
# EVERY PERMISSION BELOW WAS OBSERVED. Method: auditallow on the whole macro for one build,
# then scripts/gos-selinux.sh --granted after exercising AirDrop send, AirDrop receive,
# Quick Share off-network receive over Bluetooth, and a Quick Share receive that upgraded
# to Wi-Fi Direct. Every run loss-free (the kernel's 50/s audit rate limit has to be lifted
# first or it silently drops records and fakes a clean result) and zero denials throughout.
#
# GONE, none of it ever observed:
#   rawip_socket          anything -- the whole class
#   icmp_socket           anything -- the whole class
#   netlink_route_socket  anything -- the whole class, and its allowxperm
#   node_bind             for icmp_socket and rawip_socket
#   setattr, lock, append, map on tcp/udp
#
# netlink_route_socket is worth its own line. It is what a stale-group liveness check once
# needed, did NOT throw without -- it returned an empty interface list instead -- and so
# confidently tore down LIVE Wi-Fi Direct groups once a second. It was instrumented here
# specifically to catch anything still reading routes. Nothing does. Removing the class
# means that particular bug cannot be written again without a denial saying so.
allow tarishsharingd self:tcp_socket
    { create ioctl read write bind connect listen accept getattr getopt setopt shutdown };
allow tarishsharingd self:udp_socket
    { create ioctl read write bind getattr setopt shutdown };
allow tarishsharingd port_type:tcp_socket { name_bind name_connect };
allow tarishsharingd port_type:udp_socket name_bind;
allow tarishsharingd node_type:tcp_socket node_bind;
allow tarishsharingd node_type:udp_socket node_bind;

# netd's fwmark socket. MISSED ON THE FIRST NARROWING PASS, and the way it was missed is
# worth recording: net_domain's grants were enumerated by grepping for `allow ... netdomain`,
# but two of them are MACRO INVOCATIONS --
#     private/net.te:39  unix_socket_connect(netdomain, dnsproxyd, netd)
#     private/net.te:42  unix_socket_connect(netdomain, fwmarkd, netd)
# -- and a macro call contains no `allow`, so no such grep can see it. Enumerate a macro by
# what it EXPANDS to, not by what its rules look like.
#
# fwmark is how Android tags a socket with the network it should use; libc asks netd over
# this socket on connect() and bind(). Denied, the request fails and the socket stays
# unmarked.
#
# That is why it did not show up as a broken transfer. Quick Share over Wi-Fi Direct talks
# to 192.168.49.x on a directly-connected interface, where exactly one route matches and no
# network selection is needed -- both directions ran at full speed with this denied. It
# bites where a destination is reachable by more than one network, which means the LAN path,
# and it interacts with VPN lockdown, whose rules test fwmark 0x0/0x20000.
#
# NB an unmarked socket can never SET that bit, so the failure mode is closed, not open.
# This is a correctness fix, not a security one.
allow tarishsharingd fwmarkd_socket:sock_file write;
allow tarishsharingd netd:unix_stream_socket connectto;

# NOT granted, though net_domain did: unix_socket_connect(netdomain, dnsproxyd, netd), the
# DNS resolver socket. This daemon finds peers over mDNS and connects to addresses, never
# names, and no denial for it has appeared across AirDrop both ways and Quick Share both
# ways. A process that parses hostile input and cannot make a DNS query is a better shape,
# so this stays out until something proves it necessary -- at which point it will say so
# with an AVC naming dnsproxyd_socket.

# KEPT WITHOUT HAVING BEEN OBSERVED, and deliberately:
#   read, write, ioctl  -- not instrumented at all. They fire per buffer and would have
#                          buried the rest; a completed transfer proves them.
#   shutdown            -- instrumented and never fired, including across a real Wi-Fi
#                          Direct handover, which was the path most likely to use it
#                          (LAST_WRITE_TO_PRIOR / SAFE_TO_CLOSE_PRIOR). Two paths still
#                          have not run on this build: Quick Share over the LAN, and
#                          off-network SEND. Shutting down a socket you already own grants
#                          no reach, so keeping it costs nothing in security terms and
#                          avoids a transfer that fails at the very end on an untested path.
#                          Drop it once those two are measured.

# Publish ITarishService, and talk binder to the clients that bind it.
binder_use(tarishsharingd)
add_service(tarishsharingd, tarish_service)

# PLATFORM_APP, not system_app, and the difference is the app's uid.
#
# seapp_contexts maps a platform-signed app by the uid it runs as:
#
#     user=system seinfo=platform                          domain=system_app
#     user=_app   minTargetSdkVersion=37 seinfo=platform    domain=platform_app
#
# The app used to declare sharedUserId="android.uid.system" and so ran as uid 1000 and
# matched the first line. Dropping that -- which is the point, since it made the app the
# system user and more privileged than this daemon -- moves it to the second. Rules
# written against system_app then match nothing, and under enforcing the app cannot find
# the service at all. Under permissive it works while logging denials, which is the worst
# of the two outcomes because it looks fine.
#
# Publishing the service is not enough on its own: a client also needs `call` on this
# domain and `find` on the service name, and without them binder fails with a bare
# "remote transaction failed" that says nothing about SELinux.
#
# binder_call both ways, because the daemon calls BACK into the app through
# ITarishCallback to say files have arrived.
#
# CLOSED (security review #28). This used to read:
#
#   binder_call(platform_app, tarishsharingd)
#   binder_call(tarishsharingd, platform_app)
#   allow platform_app tarish_service:service_manager find;
#
# platform_app is a class, not this app -- every platform-signed app with its own uid
# lands in it, and each one could then drive the whole interface: enumerate peers, send
# files, watch transfers, setPolicy. The note here said narrowing it "cannot be written
# blind", and that was right: the rule set was not guessed, it was derived from real
# denials on a permissive build (tarish_app.te records which, and why each is needed).
#
# The app now has its own domain and the grants live in tarish_app.te, keyed by package
# name in seapp_contexts. Nothing else calls this service: tarishctl runs in `shell` and
# is deliberately NOT granted -- under enforcing it is refused, which is the proof that
# the lock holds.

# /proc/net/if_inet6, to find our own link-local address for the AAAA record we
# advertise. The alternative is getifaddrs(), which would mean ~30 lines of
# unsafe pointer walking in the most exposed process in Tarish -- a worse trade
# than one read-only proc file for a domain that already has network access.
allow tarishsharingd proc_net:file r_file_perms;

# Read interface state, which is how this daemon knows the AWDL link is up
# without talking to tarishd or holding any privilege.
allow tarishsharingd sysfs_net:dir search;
allow tarishsharingd sysfs_net:file r_file_perms;

# Where received archives are stored, before the app collects them.
#
# The daemon writes files here and nothing else: it does not read them back, and it
# cannot reach shared storage at all. Downloads/Tarish is the app's job, because the app
# is the side with the standing to write there and to tell MediaStore about it -- and
# because parsing a hostile archive must not happen in a process that has that standing.
type tarish_data_file, file_type, data_file_type, core_data_file_type;
allow tarishsharingd tarish_data_file:dir { search write add_name getattr };
allow tarishsharingd tarish_data_file:file { create write open getattr };

# Listing the inbox, and reading an archive back to extract it.
#
# Both were missing and both were derived from real denials rather than guessed:
# read_dir needs `open` on the directory as well as `read`, and the extractor reopens
# the .cpio it just wrote. The symptom was a transfer that arrived and then never
# completed -- the bytes landed, extraction failed silently, and nothing appeared.
allow tarishsharingd tarish_data_file:dir { read open };
allow tarishsharingd tarish_data_file:file read;

# MOVED to tarish_app.te (security review #28). This used to read:
#
#   allow platform_app tarish_data_file:file read;
#   allow platform_app tarish_data_file:dir search;
#
# which let ANY platform-signed app read the contents of every received file. That is a
# worse grant than the binder one above it, and easier to miss, because it does not
# mention the service at all.
#
# The `file read` half is real and now belongs to tarish_app alone. The `dir search` half
# was dropped: a permissive run of a whole AirDrop receive logged only the file read, so
# nothing walks the inbox by path any more, and the app should not be able to.

# And the reverse, for sending: the client passes descriptors for files it opened from
# MediaStore, so this daemon has to be allowed to use an fd that mediaprovider owns.
# Without it, sharing anything from the gallery fails before a byte is read.
allow tarishsharingd mediaprovider_app:fd use;

# ...and read the file behind that descriptor. Files the user picks live on the FUSE
# view of external storage, so they are labelled `fuse` rather than anything of ours.
# Being allowed to USE a descriptor is not the same as being allowed to READ what it
# points at, which is the distinction that broke sending: the daemon took the fd and
# then got EACCES on the first byte.
allow tarishsharingd fuse:file { read getattr };

# Removing an archive once it is extracted, and a received file once the client has
# stored it. Writing into a directory does not imply being allowed to take things out
# of it -- unlink needs remove_name on the directory as well as the file.
allow tarishsharingd tarish_data_file:dir remove_name;
allow tarishsharingd tarish_data_file:file unlink;

# NOT granted, and each absence is deliberate:
#   no capability of any kind
#   no netlink_generic_socket   -- driving the radio is tarishd's job
#   no packet_socket            -- raw frames are tarishd's job
#   no tun_device               -- creating tlink0 is tarishd's job
#   no execute of anything
#   no sdcard / media_rw access -- Downloads/Tarish is reached by the app, never here

# Telling tarishd whether the AWDL radio is wanted.
#
# This is the ONLY property this domain may set, and it carries one boolean. The
# asymmetry is deliberate: the unprivileged half decides *whether* the radio is
# needed, and the privileged half decides *how* to bring it up. Nothing about the
# channel, the country or the interface crosses this boundary.
#
# Denied, tarishd keeps the radio up and Tarish still works -- see WANT_PROP. So a
# missing rule here shows up as battery life, not as a broken transfer, which is
# exactly the failure mode to prefer but also an easy one to miss.
set_prop(tarishsharingd, tarish_awdl_prop)

# The device name. The app cannot write this itself -- a persist property needs a
# grant an app should not have -- so it arrives over binder and is written here.
set_prop(tarishsharingd, tarish_name_prop)

# Use a socket the APP connected and passed over binder.
#
# Reaching a Quick Share peer with no network means Bluetooth, and framework Bluetooth is
# unreachable from a native service. So the app opens the connection and hands the
# descriptor across; this daemon runs the protocol on it. Without this rule the binder
# transaction fails and the app sees DeadObjectException -- "remote process probably
# died" -- while the daemon is alive and well and never hears about the call. The denial
# names sockfs and gives no hint that a policy line is missing.
#
# read/write only. NOT create, connect or bind: this daemon must never be able to open a
# socket of its own from a descriptor someone else handed it, and it has no reason to --
# the app already connected it.
#
# ONE RULE PER SOCKET CLASS, and that is the part that caught us out twice. The note above
# was already written when a Wi-Fi Direct upgrade started passing a TCP socket instead of a
# socketpair, and it failed in EXACTLY the same way -- DeadObjectException in the app, a
# daemon that never hears the call -- because the class is different and the rule below did
# not cover it:
#
#   avc: denied { read write } for path="socket:[82541]" dev="sockfs"
#     scontext=u:r:tarishsharingd:s0 tcontext=u:r:platform_app:s0 tclass=tcp_socket
#
# The denial went to LOGCAT and not to dmesg -- `dmesg | grep -c avc` was 0 while logcat
# had it -- so a dmesg-only look says the transaction failed for no reason at all.
#
# These name tarish_app, not platform_app (security review #28) -- the sockets come from
# OUR app and no other, so the daemon should only be able to work a descriptor that app
# passed it.
allow tarishsharingd tarish_app:unix_stream_socket { read write getattr };
# The Wi-Fi Direct / hotspot upgrade: the app joins the group, connects to the group owner
# and passes this across. setopt because the protocol sets TCP_NODELAY and a read timeout
# on it; shutdown so the transfer can end the connection rather than only closing its own
# descriptor.
allow tarishsharingd tarish_app:tcp_socket { read write getattr getopt setopt shutdown };

# set_prop() alone is NOT enough on this release. The macro grants
# `tarish_awdl_prop:property_service set` and `init:unix_stream_socket connectto`, but
# libc still writes to /dev/socket/property_service first, and that write is checked
# against the SOCKET's label, not the property's:
#
#   avc: denied { write } for name="property_service" scontext=u:r:tarishsharingd:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:property_socket:s0 tclass=sock_file permissive=0
#
# Without this the radio gate fails exactly as the note above predicted: every flip is
# refused, tarish.awdl.wanted never changes, tarishd holds the radio forever, and the only
# symptom is battery. Measured on mustang under enforcing -- tarishsharingd had never
# once logged a successful flip.
allow tarishsharingd property_socket:sock_file write;
